{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>But you speak to us</b> — \"You have weakened my strength like a woman, for I was troubled on your account and you slackened my hands when I saw that you were not zealous to draw near to Him out of love, and was it not fine to learn from the mouth of the Gevurah and not from me?\" — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 5:24), from aggadic teachings. And if so, he says that Moses worried for them, but HaShem informed him that they acted out of fear and that it was good for them. And the matter is as I explained there (Exodus 20:15), that they thought He wished to make them hear the entire Torah, but His will was to make them hear only the Ten Commandments, just as they requested. \n\nKnow that now they requested of Moses that they not hear the voice of the honored HaShem, neither on this day nor in any generation forever, for a miracle does not occur at every moment, and they do not desire such a standing; rather, they will believe in the prophets and do all the will of HaShem according to their word. And so Moses says to them later on (Deuteronomy 18:15–16): \"A prophet from among you, from your brothers, like me, will HaShem your Elohim raise up for you — to him you shall listen, according to all that you asked of HaShem your Elohim at Horeb…\" and that entire passage. If so, this request was for all generations."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "I rendered הגבורה (literally 'the Mighty One') as 'the Gevurah' in keeping with Rashi's quoted phrase, treating it as a divine epithet; Ramban quotes it as Rashi's own language."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:46:26.668926664Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}